---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Code Review - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="development" subChapterId="code-review">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				When AI writes most of the code, code review becomes the primary place
				where you exercise engineering judgment. The volume of code you need to
				review goes up, and your familiarity with each line goes down. That
				combination means review needs to be more deliberate than ever.
			</p>

			<h3>Review in Multiple Places</h3>

			<p>
				Do not rely on a single view of the changes. Use at least two:
			</p>

			<ul class="space-y-3 my-4">
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Your Git client.</span>{" "}
					Review the diff locally, file by file. This gives you the best context
					for understanding how changes fit into the surrounding code. Most Git
					clients let you stage or discard individual hunks, which is useful when
					the AI included changes you did not ask for.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">The PR page on GitHub (or your platform).</span>{" "}
					The PR view shows you CI results, automated checks, and the full scope
					of the change in one place. It also gives you a different visual layout
					than your local client, which helps you catch things you might skim past
					in a familiar environment.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h3>What to Look For</h3>

			<p>
				Your review checklist with AI-generated code is the same as any code
				review, but certain areas deserve extra attention:
			</p>

			<ul class="space-y-3 my-4">
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Does it actually solve the problem?</span>{" "}
					AI can produce working code that does the wrong thing. Verify the behavior,
					not just the syntax.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Are there security issues?</span>{" "}
					AI models do not always follow security best practices. Check for injection
					vulnerabilities, exposed secrets, missing auth checks, and unsafe defaults.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Is it consistent with the codebase?</span>{" "}
					Watch for naming inconsistencies, architectural deviations, and patterns
					that do not match the surrounding code.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Did it over-engineer?</span>{" "}
					AI often adds unnecessary abstractions, extra configuration options, or
					defensive code for scenarios that cannot happen. Simpler is better.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h3>Let AI Review Before You Send</h3>

			<p>
				Before you commit or open a PR, have an AI do a first review pass. Most tools now ship a command or skill for this. In Claude Code,{" "}
				<code>/code-review</code> checks your working diff for bugs and cleanups and{" "}
				<code>/security-review</code> scans the pending changes for vulnerabilities, both from the terminal before you push. Cursor and other tools have
				similar "review the changes" features, and you can wrap your team's standards into a custom review skill so every pass checks the same things.
				See Claude Code's{" "}
				<a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/code-review" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">code review docs</a>{" "}
				for what the local command covers.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="warning" title="This does not replace human review">
				<p>
					An AI reviewing AI-generated code shares the same blind spots that produced the code. It is good at catching obvious bugs, security smells,
					and inconsistencies, so it clears the easy problems before a person looks. It is not a substitute for your judgment. Treat it as a first pass
					that makes your review more productive, not as the review itself.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>AI Reviewers on the Pull Request</h3>

			<p>
				Depending on your project, the PR itself may get an automated AI review. Tools like{" "}
				<a href="https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/code-review" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Copilot code review</a>,{" "}
				<a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/code-review" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Claude Code's Code Review</a>,{" "}
				<a href="https://www.coderabbit.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CodeRabbit</a>, and Cursor's Bugbot post inline comments on the
				diff, tagged by severity, when a PR opens or on every push. Most of them leave a plain "Comment" review rather than an approval, so they add a
				second set of eyes without approving or blocking the merge on their own.
			</p>

			<p>
				Handle these the way you would a junior reviewer's comments: useful signal, not a verdict. Triage the findings, fix what is real, dismiss the
				noise, and still get a human approval before merging. If your team uses one, tune it with a config file (for example a{" "}
				<code>REVIEW.md</code> or <code>CLAUDE.md</code>) so it flags what matters for your codebase and stays quiet on the rest.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					If a diff is large, review it in smaller logical chunks. Think about maybe breaking up tasks into multiple PRs as you develop them.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
